{"text": "GOAL:\n\nTo determine whether exposure to cadmium aerosols induces the synthesis of\nmetallothionein in free alveolar cells recovered by broncho-pulnonary lavage.\n\nMETHODOLOGY :AND RESULTS:\n\nLewis rats were exposed 10 times to 1600 ug Cd/m\u00ae. Three days after the .\nlast exposure, they were sacrificed and their lungs were lavaged. A total\nof 56.4 x 106 cells were recovered fron animals. Differential staining\nshowed that two cell types were present in the cell population. Approximately\n40% of the cells were polymorphonuclear leucocytes and the rest were alveolar\nmacrophages. The cetls were washed in Tris buffer (pll 8.6, 0.01 M) and frozen\nat - 16 \u20ac. The next day they were thawed and honogenized.' Following centrifugation\nat 34,000 x g and a heat step (1 min at 100 C), the sample was spun at 100,000 x g\ngg 1 hour. \u2018The supernatant fluid that resulted was divided into 2 aliquots;\nCd and 0.005 n moles or 0.05 n moles of Cd was added. The cell extract was\nthen allowed to equilibrate for 1 hour at room temperature prior to fractinnation\non a Sephadex G-75 column.\n\nFigures 1 A \u00a7 B shows the results of this experiment. A component corresponding\nto a molecular weight of about 11,000 was observed in these cell extracts. It was\npossible to saturate the cadmium binding component using 0.02 n moles of Cd but not |\nwith 0.005 n moles of Cd.. It was estimated that 1.39 f moles of Cd-metallothionein\nwere present in 107 cells. Density gradient centrifugation techniques will be\nemployed to separate the alveolar macrophages from the polymorphonuclear leucocytes\no that we will be able to determine the contribution of each of these cell types\nto metallothionein synthesis. Recently SIGMA Chemical Company has announced the\nintroduction of a new density medium, listopaque 1119, which when used along with\nits companion product, Histopaque 1077, permits similtaneous isolation of three\nlayers consisting of: mononuclear cells, granulocytes, and erythrocytes.\n\nGOAL:\n\nTo determine whether animals develop tolerance to sub-chronic exposure to cadnium.\n\nMETHODOLOGY AND RESULTS\n\nPreliminary experiments were performed to determine whether multiple inhalation\nexposures to cadnium lead to the development of increased pulmonary tolerance to\ncadnium. Rats were exposed between 1 and 24 times to 1600 ug Cd/m> for 3 hr periods.\nSeventy-two hours after the requisite mumber of exposures, the animals were sacrificed.\nLung damage was assessed by: (1) Cytological Studies in which the total number and\nKind of cells present in the alveolar conpartnent were determined and (2) Biochemical\nStudies in which cell-free lavage fluid was analyzed for tissue enzyme level\n\nThe results of the cytological studies are shown on Pig. 5. Normally less than 1%\nof the free alveolar cell population consists of polymorphonuclear leucocytes and\nlymphocytes. However, after 1 exposure to cadmium, there is a significant increase,\nin polynorphonuclear leucocytes. The nunber of PMNs continues to rise and reaches\na peak level after 12 exposures to cadmium; in subsequent exposures, the number of\nPMNs begins to decline. In addition to PMNs there is a small yet significant increase\nin the number of lymphocytes present follwing Cd exposure. The lymphocytic infiltration\npeaks after 5 exp ures and then begins to decline. The nunber of alveolar macrophages\nincreases about 2 fold in response to cadmium exposure and remains at this slightly\nincreased level.\n\n50233589",
